---
import PageFrame from "../components/PageFrame.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Download Logos AI"
  description="Download the desktop app for the richest Logos AI workflow, including offline-friendly reading, voices, and AI study tools."
>
  <PageFrame current="download">
    <main class="shell py-16">
      <section class="max-w-4xl animate-rise">
        <p class="eyebrow">Download</p>
        <h1 class="mt-4 section-title">The desktop app is the flagship experience.</h1>
        <p class="mt-6 section-copy">
          The browser is the low-friction front door. The downloaded app is where read-aloud, AI commentary, offline
          workflows, richer search, and future sermon or church features can grow without compromise.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <article class="surface-card p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">macOS</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl text-text">Tray-first setup</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-7 text-muted">
            Best fit for the current Wails build. Use the menu bar flow, keep the reader close at hand, and move into
            deeper study without keeping a heavy browser tab open.
          </p>
          <a href="https://github.com/jd4rider/logos/releases" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="mt-6 inline-flex cta-primary">
            View releases
          </a>
        </article>

        <article class="surface-card p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">Windows</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl text-text">Study desk</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-7 text-muted">
            Use the desktop build when you want richer reading, AI help, and future exports or sermon prep in a more
            persistent workspace.
          </p>
          <a href="https://github.com/jd4rider/logos/releases" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="mt-6 inline-flex cta-secondary">
            Release page
          </a>
        </article>

        <article class="surface-card p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">Linux</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl text-text">Power-user path</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-7 text-muted">
            Ideal for technical users who care about local models, offline workflows, CLI/TUI flexibility, and direct
            configuration control.
          </p>
          <a href="https://github.com/jd4rider/logos/releases" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="mt-6 inline-flex cta-secondary">
            Release page
          </a>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="surface-card p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">What you get</p>
          <ul class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-sm leading-7 text-muted">
            <li>Offline-friendly reading and imported local translations</li>
            <li>AI commentary and study help where configured</li>
            <li>Read-aloud workflows and premium voices later</li>
            <li>Future sermon prep, exports, sync, and team features</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="surface-card p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">Setup</p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-7 text-muted">
            Documentation for BYOK, local setup, and managed-mode expectations lives on the docs site. The goal is to
            keep the onboarding clear whether someone wants a no-setup path or full control.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
            <a href="https://docs.logos-ai.online" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="cta-primary">Open docs</a>
            <a href="/try" class="cta-secondary">Try web demo</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
  </PageFrame>
</SiteLayout>
